---
import LearningLayout from '../../../layouts/LearningLayout.astro';
import ForestPlot from '../../../components/ForestPlot.astro';
import Icon from '../../../components/Icon.astro';
import { foodAnalysis, foodSyntheses, foodStudies } from '../../../lib/food-evidence';
import { safeJson } from '../../../lib/evidence';
const main = foodSyntheses[0];
---
<LearningLayout title="Compare the effect estimates" section="effects" intro="Six fictional household-randomised trials. One specified purchasing outcome. Inspect each estimate before looking at the pooled result.">
  <div class="learning-notice"><Icon name="info"/><div><strong>Calculated from synthetic counts, not real studies.</strong><p>These numbers are not evidence about any retailer, programme or population.</p></div></div>
  <section class="analysis-controls"><div><label for="outcome-select">Outcome</label><select id="outcome-select" disabled><option value="purchase">Basket purchase within four weeks</option><option value="health">Diet quality or health outcomes</option></select></div><div><label for="analysis-select">Analysis specification</label><select id="analysis-select" disabled><option value="all">All six fictional trials</option><option value="lower-risk">Exclude the high-risk trial</option></select></div><label class="checkbox-label"><input type="checkbox" id="show-pooled" checked disabled/> Show pooled estimate</label></section>
  <noscript><p class="notice">The main analysis and source links are readable without JavaScript. Enable JavaScript to switch analyses, hide the pooled estimate or change the assumed baseline.</p></noscript>
  <p class="analysis-live" id="analysis-status" role="status" aria-live="polite">All six fictional trials. The pooled estimate uses only this specified set.</p>
  <div id="unmeasured-outcome" class="unmeasured-panel" hidden><h2>This outcome was not measured.</h2><p>The six fictional trials report purchasing a specified basket. They do not report diet quality or health outcomes. No effect or pooled estimate can be shown for these outcomes.</p><a class="text-link" href="/learning/food-access/context/">See the reporting gaps <Icon/></a></div>
  <div id="quantitative-results">
  {foodSyntheses.map(synthesis=>{const studies=foodStudies.filter(s=>synthesis.studyIds.includes(s.id));return <section data-analysis={synthesis.id} hidden={synthesis.id!=='all'}><div class="analysis-metrics"><div data-pooled-summary><span class="metric-label">Synthetic pooled risk ratio</span><strong>{synthesis.rr.toFixed(2)}</strong><span>95% CI {synthesis.ciLow.toFixed(2)}–{synthesis.ciHigh.toFixed(2)}</span></div><div><span class="metric-label">Included evidence</span><strong>{synthesis.k} <small>trials</small></strong><span>{synthesis.totalParticipants.toLocaleString('en-GB')} households across both arms</span></div><div><span class="metric-label">Heterogeneity</span><strong>{synthesis.i2.toFixed(0)}<small>% I²</small></strong><span>τ² = {synthesis.tau2.toFixed(4)} · not a quality score</span></div></div><div class="chart-panel"><div class="chart-panel-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Outcome: at least one basket purchase</p><h2>{synthesis.id==='all'?'The prespecified main analysis':'Prespecified sensitivity analysis'}</h2></div><span class="small">Four-week follow-up</span></div><ForestPlot studies={studies} synthesis={synthesis}/></div>{synthesis.id==='lower-risk'&&<p class="notice">Trial 06 is excluded because its fictional appraisal identifies high attrition and post-randomisation exclusions. This is a planned sensitivity check, not a new certainty rating. Remaining studies still have limitations.</p>}<div class="data-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Effect estimates table"><table class="learning-table"><caption>Extracted synthetic counts and calculated estimates — {synthesis.id==='all'?'all trials':'excluding Trial 06'}</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Study and source</th><th scope="col">Offer: purchased / total</th><th scope="col">Usual offer: purchased / total</th><th scope="col">Risk ratio (95% CI)</th><th scope="col">Example risk of bias</th></tr></thead><tbody>{studies.map(study=><tr><th scope="row"><a href={`/learning/food-access/studies/${study.id}/`}>Trial {study.id.slice(2,4)} · {study.year} ↗</a></th><td>{study.interventionEvents} / {study.interventionTotal}</td><td>{study.controlEvents} / {study.controlTotal}</td><td>{study.effect.rr.toFixed(2)} ({study.effect.ciLow.toFixed(2)}–{study.effect.ciHigh.toFixed(2)})</td><td>{study.riskOfBias.replaceAll('-',' ')}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div></section>})}
  <section class="absolute-panel" id="absolute-panel"><div><p class="eyebrow">Translate the relative effect</p><h2>What would this mean per 100 households?</h2><p>Choose an assumed usual-offer purchasing rate. This is a conditional arithmetic illustration, not a local prediction.</p><label for="baseline-risk">Assumed usual-offer rate: <output id="baseline-risk-value" for="baseline-risk">30</output> per 100 households</label><input id="baseline-risk" type="range" min="10" max="50" step="5" value="30" disabled/></div><div class="absolute-result" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"><strong id="absolute-rate">{(30*main.rr).toFixed(1)}</strong><span>per 100 with the offer</span><p id="absolute-ci">{(30*main.ciLow).toFixed(1)}–{(30*main.ciHigh).toFixed(1)} per 100 using the pooled confidence limits</p><p id="absolute-difference">{Math.abs(30*(main.rr-1)).toFixed(1)} {main.rr>=1?'more':'fewer'} per 100 under these assumptions</p></div><p class="small full-width">Calculation: assumed baseline risk × selected pooled risk ratio. The range transforms only the effect-estimate confidence limits; it does not include uncertainty in the assumed baseline, transportability or the model. No purchasing estimate is transferred into the access simulation.</p></section>
  </div>
  <section class="learning-section"><details class="methods-disclosure"><summary>Analysis specification and reproducibility</summary><div><p><strong>Eligibility:</strong> six invented individually randomised household trials, a household-specific basket voucher and personalised information versus the usual offer, the same binary purchasing outcome, and four-week follow-up. These are not shop-cluster trials.</p><p><strong>Method:</strong> log risk ratios with within-study variance calculated from the two-arm event counts; inverse-variance random-effects meta-analysis with the Paule–Mandel estimator. Conventional normal 95% confidence intervals are used, without a Hartung–Knapp adjustment. This is an illustrative choice, not a prescribed method for the commissioned review.</p><p><strong>Multiple reports:</strong> each row represents one independent synthetic trial. In a real review, reports from the same study would share a study identifier.</p><p><strong>Computation:</strong> {foodAnalysis.software}. Results are generated by a reproducible Python script before publication. The browser selects between two existing analysis specifications; it does not recalculate a pooled result from arbitrary filters.</p><p><strong>Limitations:</strong> six hand-authored trials are not a searched evidence base; risk-of-bias flags are illustrative; model assumptions and the treatment of small-study uncertainty would need review for any real analysis.</p></div></details></section>
  <div class="learning-endlinks"><a href="/data/food-analysis.json" download>Download results and inputs <Icon name="download" size={17}/></a><a href="/learning/food-access/confidence/">Read the confidence profile <Icon size={17}/></a></div>
  <script id="food-analysis-data" type="application/json" set:html={safeJson(foodAnalysis)}/>
  <script>import '../../../scripts/effects';</script>
</LearningLayout>
